  "verbalization": "Regorafenib is classified as a Cytochrome P450 2c9 (CYP2C9) inhibitor, and this classification is supported by multiple evidence paths. The mechanism of action involves Regorafenib binding to CYP2C8, as evidenced by the interactions between Regorafenib and Zafirlukast, and between Regorafenib and Amiodarone, both of which are also CYP2C9 inhibitors. The lowest common ancestor of these interactions is the class of Organic Heterocyclic Compounds, suggesting a shared biological pathway. The integrated evidence suggests that Regorafenib may inhibit CYP2C8 through a common mechanism, potentially affecting drug metabolism. The scores from the paths indicate a moderate level of confidence in this relationship, but further validation would be needed to confirm the direct interaction between Regorafenib and CYP2C8.",
